boxing: Boxed variables

A local variable lives on the C++ stack unless something can see it after it goes out of
scope, or from another function while it is in scope. Then it is boxed: the C++ variable is
a gp::ptr<T> to a heap block (gp::new_<T>(...)), each use of x is (*x), and &x is the box
itself. A pointer to a variable never dangles: it shares the box, which lives as long as the
last pointer.

Taking the address is not enough to box a variable. The escape analysis (ADR-114) looks at
every use of the address: if the pointer is only read and written through, compared, or
handed to a function that borrows it, then x stays an ordinary C++ variable, a pointer to it
is gp::borrow(&x) or gp::stack_ptr(x) with no heap block and no count, and the note says
that it stays on the C++ stack. This holds for a pointer method called on x (x.Inc()), for p
:= &x, for &T{...} and new(T) in a local pointer, and for a variable that only closures
called where they are made capture (not a parameter or a result: those keep their box).

A variable is boxed when its address (&x, &x.f, &x.arr[i]) is stored, returned, sent,
captured, converted to an interface, passed to a function that keeps it, or used by go or
defer; when it slices an array in it; when a closure that can outlive the call captures it
and changes it anywhere; when a closure captures it and it is larger than 128 bytes; when it
is larger than 128 KiB; or when keeping it on the stack would pass the budget (2 KiB for one
object, 16 KiB for one function). Function parameters, results and loop variables are boxed
whenever their address is taken, and the generic functions are not analysed: they box as
before. The first rule that applies is the reason in the annotation.

gocpp build -gp:m prints the decisions like go build -gcflags=-m (-gp:m=2 adds the reasons),
and -gp:noescape turns the analysis off, so that every variable whose address is taken is
boxed.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#pointers-and-boxing
See also: gocpp explain closures, gocpp explain refcount, gocpp explain allocation
Also known as: box, boxed

Example (boxing.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    type Counter struct{ n int }

    func (c *Counter) Inc() { c.n++ }

    func newCounter() *Counter {
    	c := Counter{n: 1} // its address is returned: boxed
    	return &c
    }

    func main() {
    	var local Counter // a pointer method is called on it: not boxed
    	local.Inc()
    	x := 41 // nothing sees x after the function: it stays on the stack
    	x++
    	p := newCounter()
    	p.Inc()
    	fmt.Println(local.n, x, p.n)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    struct Counter;

    struct Counter {
        int64_t n = 0;
        bool operator==(const Counter&) const = default;
        std::size_t gp_hash() const {
            return gp::hash_fields(n);
        }
        static constexpr auto gp_fields = gp::fields(gp::field(&Counter::n, "n", false, false));
        static void Inc(gp::bptr<Counter> c);
    };
    static_assert(sizeof(Counter) == 8 && alignof(Counter) == 8);

    gp::ptr<Counter> newCounter();
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/boxing.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/boxing.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:7: func (c *Counter) Inc() { c.n++ }
    // gp[refcount] col 7: c is borrowed: the body only reads and writes through it, compares it or
    //     lends it on, so the C++ parameter is a raw gp::bptr<T> and a call increments no reference
    //     count
    // gp[checks] col 29: p.f through a pointer: -> on a gp::ptr checks for nil and panics like Go
    //     (gocpp build -nochecks removes it)
    void Counter::Inc(gp::bptr<Counter> c) {
        c->n++;
    }

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:9: func newCounter() *Counter {
    gp::ptr<Counter> newCounter() {

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:10: c := Counter{n: 1} // its address is returned: boxed
    // gp[boxing] col 2: c is boxed: its address (or the address of a part of it) is taken, so it lives
    //     in a gp::ptr heap box that every pointer shares and that never dangles
        gp::ptr<Counter> c = gp::new_<Counter>(Counter{.n = 1});

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:11: return &c
    // gp[refcount] col 9: &c is the box of c itself: the pointer shares its reference count, so the
    //     variable lives as long as any pointer to it
        return c;

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:12: }
    }

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:14: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:15: var local Counter // a pointer method is called on it: not boxed
    // gp[boxing] col 6: local stays on the C++ stack: its address is taken, but the escape analysis
    //     found that the address does not escape, so a pointer to it is gp::borrow(&local) or a
    //     gp::stack_ptr value (no heap box, no counts)
        Counter local{};

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:16: local.Inc()
    // gp[refcount] col 2: lent to Inc as the raw address of a part of an object that stays alive during
    //     the call: no aliasing pointer, no reference count (gp::borrow)
    // gp[methods] col 2: pointer method Inc: a static call T::Inc(p, ...), the receiver pointer is the
    //     first argument; no dynamic dispatch
        Counter::Inc(gp::borrow(&local));

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:17: x := 41 // nothing sees x after the function: it stays on the stack
        int64_t x = 41;

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:18: x++
        x++;

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:19: p := newCounter()
        gp::ptr<Counter> p = newCounter();

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:20: p.Inc()
    // gp[refcount] col 2: lent to Inc without a reference count: a temporary, or a variable that no
    //     call can change, keeps the object alive (gp::lend)
    // gp[methods] col 2: pointer method Inc: a static call T::Inc(p, ...), the receiver pointer is the
    //     first argument; no dynamic dispatch
        Counter::Inc(gp::lend(p));

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:21: fmt.Println(local.n, x, p.n)
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
    // gp[checks] col 28: p.f through a pointer: -> on a gp::ptr checks for nil and panics like Go
    //     (gocpp build -nochecks removes it)
        gp::fmt::Println(local.n, x, p->n);

    // ---- ./boxing.gp:22: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
